WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:02.380
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello, again.

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:03.300
I'm Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.930
I'm joined now by Yoshi Tamura,
Product Manager at Google.

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.020
And we are here to
talk about gVisor.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.460
So gVisor-- brand new,
just open-sourced this week

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:17.940
at KubeCon here in Copenhagen.
Give me the highlights

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:18.900
of that announcement.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.080
YOSHI TAMURA: Yes, so Google
Cloud just announced gVisor.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:25.710
It is a new open-source
sandbox technology

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.170
for container isolation.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:33.210
It is very lightweight, yet
provide a very strong isolation

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:35.062
compared to various
[INAUDIBLE] approaches.

00:00:35.062 --> 00:00:36.270
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Interesting.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.950
So you said it's new.

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.080
It's new to the
open-source community.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.630
This is technology that's
been in use at Google

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:43.800
for quite some time, right?

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.820
YOSHI TAMURA: Correct.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.540
We have been using gVisor
at Google for a while.

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.380
And we're very excited to
share the technology and also

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:54.200
the knowledge that
we gain from there.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.725
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Yeah, so why are we

00:00:55.725 --> 00:00:58.830
at Google open-sourcing
this project now?

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:01.650
YOSHI TAMURA: It is mainly
because container adoption--

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.210
Docker, Kubernetes.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:05.670
Now, a lot of people
are using containers.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:07.320
And the more people
use containers,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.410
we started seeing a
lot of new use cases,

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:13.620
especially multi-tenancy,
where there's

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:17.850
one cluster with different types
of groups or even customers.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.250
Now, the container
isolation becomes

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.040
more important in this
kind of situation.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:27.840
We believe that
open-sourcing gVisor, which

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.800
is a new proposal to the
open-source community

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.755
could advance the technology in
this container isolation space.

00:01:33.755 --> 00:01:35.880
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So it's
really about the security.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.420
It's about sandboxing some
containers-- all containers?

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.230
Some containers?

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.290
YOSHI TAMURA: Specifically on--
you could definitely specify.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.010
But if you specify that
you want stronger isolation

00:01:46.010 --> 00:01:47.819
for a particular
container, yes, you

00:01:47.819 --> 00:01:49.110
can actually do the [? show. ?]

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:49.380
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.240
And how strong is
this isolation?

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.430
Is it as strong as a VM?

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.130
YOSHI TAMURA: We believe that
is kind of a very closer level,

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.140
for sure.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.770
The other aspect,
I think, strong

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.770
and the lightweight part.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:03.690
In our testing
environment, it can

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.417
launch within 150 millisecond.

00:02:06.417 --> 00:02:08.250
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: 150
milliseconds to launch?

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.166
YOSHI TAMURA: Exactly.

00:02:09.166 --> 00:02:12.090
And also, the footprint
is about 15 megabytes.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.802
So it is very lightweight, and
yet provide a strong isolation.

00:02:14.802 --> 00:02:16.260
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Super lightweight.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:23.610
So compare that lightweight
to, say, QMU, KVM, Kata.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.730
YOSHI TAMURA: Yes, so there
are existing approaches

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.980
like the ones that
you mentioned.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.200
You can definitely
put the container

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.330
inside a virtual
machine to provide

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:34.457
a very strong isolation.

00:02:34.457 --> 00:02:35.290
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: OK.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:37.080
Is that what gVisor is?

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.030
YOSHI TAMURA: It is
actually very similar.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.550
But if you look into the
detail where you can look--

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:42.840
please look at the blog.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.110
It actually highlights
the key differences there.

00:02:46.110 --> 00:02:48.900
But in a nutshell,
the virtual machine,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.990
you actually need to
run, QMU [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:51.990 --> 00:02:54.630
device [? simulate ?] and
also the guest kernel.

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.840
Therefore the footprint plus
also the attacking surface

00:02:57.840 --> 00:02:59.520
is still significant enough.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.100
gVisor took a little
bit different approach,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.120
where you trap the system
calls and then provide

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.410
multiple layers of security.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.452
This is, I think, the
great part of the gVisor.

00:03:10.452 --> 00:03:11.910
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Yeah, interesting.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:17.850
So do you actually run a kernel
inside of the gVisor machine?

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.170
YOSHI TAMURA: It is a
very tricky question.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:22.440
So I strongly recommend
going to the blog.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.572
It's a very novel and innovative
approach that I think is--

00:03:25.572 --> 00:03:27.780
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Sys calls
are being trapped, though?

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:28.170
YOSHI TAMURA: Yes.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.670
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
And those are being

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:31.200
executed by the host kernel.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.830
YOSHI TAMURA: Actually, so
there is a guest user LAN

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.140
kernel that will drive this.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.520
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Microkernel.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.061
YOSHI TAMURA: It is
not a microkernel

00:03:40.061 --> 00:03:42.800
from a technical perspective.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.530
Again, it's a very new approach
and it's very creative.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:49.860
We also reached out to the Kata
container community about this.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.840
They were also very welcome.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.360
And as you can see
in a blog post,

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.150
we also got the quotes
from their team.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:56.650
So I think--

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:57.030
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
You think there's

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:58.780
an opportunity for
the two projects

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.620
to really come together and
advance the state of the art?

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:04.410
YOSHI TAMURA: I think that
is the most exciting part.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.140
This feels so important
for our ecosystem.

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:09.690
And taking different
approaches is definitely

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.090
going to advance the
field altogether.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.970
So that is the most exciting
part of why we're open-sourcing

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:16.596
and what we look for
toward the future.

00:04:16.596 --> 00:04:19.220
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And if someone
wants to get started with this,

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.399
what's the best
place to get started?

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:24.390
So we also have, I guess,
the Getting Started section

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:25.660
in our blog.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:26.660
But, certainly, yes.

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:30.920
Going from GitHub, you can
download the source code.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.790
gVisor is implementing OCI.

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:36.960
Therefore, it runs on with
Kubernetes and Docker.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.784
So if you're interested, just
go to GitHub and try out.

00:04:40.784 --> 00:04:42.450
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So
it's another runtime

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:45.220
that Kubernetes supports,
or will support.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:46.720
YOSHI TAMURA: So
the runtime itself,

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.810
[? or ?] command is
call runsc, which

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:51.890
is where you can replace
the existing runc

00:04:51.890 --> 00:04:53.610
and drop in a replacement.

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.090
So you are familiar
with those concepts,

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:57.840
you should be able to
run it very quickly.

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:58.756
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Sure.

00:04:58.756 --> 00:05:01.310
And if I do this, can
I run some containers

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:05.780
in the same Kubernetes with
runc and some containers

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.370
at a runsc context?

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.630
YOSHI TAMURA: Right now,
the Kubernetes community,

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.410
especially, [? should ?]
know that we're specifying

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.030
the higher-level API
[? call ?] sandbox.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.780
So until that gets
into Kubernetes,

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:21.200
you may have to [INAUDIBLE] some
manual points to control that.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:23.210
But we believe
that integration is

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:25.931
going to come to the
Kubernetes world very quickly.

00:05:25.931 --> 00:05:27.180
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: That's great.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:28.080
That's great.

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.260
So I've got a question for you.

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.420
How did you get
started in Kubernetes?

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:33.920
YOSHI TAMURA: It's
a great question.

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:36.080
I will say two points.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:39.350
When I joined Google,
I was very excited

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.600
about the internal
system called Borg.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.400
So Kubernetes, based off of
Borg or derived from Borg

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.200
is some kind of a
way that I got more--

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:52.790
I got the knowledge
after getting Google.

00:05:52.790 --> 00:05:54.970
The other aspect,
which is interesting,

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:57.470
is before I joined
the Kubernetes team,

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.000
I went to Tokyo.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:01.870
One of my friends were
saying, I'm using Kubernetes.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:04.530
I actually didn't know about
Kubernetes at that time.

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:09.320
So that was how I learned
about Kubernetes in 2017.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:10.580
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Interesting.

00:06:10.580 --> 00:06:13.157
And another question for you.

00:06:13.157 --> 00:06:14.990
You're involved in the
Kubernetes community.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:18.680
Certainly encounter a
lot of people in our very

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:21.260
large and open community.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:24.440
Can you tell us a few of the
people that inspired you,

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:28.080
that you find yourself working
with and being super impressed,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.810
and appreciative of?

00:06:29.810 --> 00:06:31.460
YOSHI TAMURA: Absolutely.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:35.240
A little bit hesitating
to highlight my colleague

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.670
at Google, but it is
definitely a truth,

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:42.500
so Dawn Chen has been
inspiring me quite a bit.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:48.470
I work with her on gVisor, and
also various Kubernetes stuff.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:52.050
She has a very strong
and visionary technical

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:55.290
insight, and also very
good communication.

00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:57.422
So it's just a joy
to work with her.

00:06:57.422 --> 00:06:58.630
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Well, great.

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:00.020
Yoshi, thank you very much.

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:01.340
YOSHI TAMURA: Thank
you very much, Dustin.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:02.589
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Domo arigato.

00:07:02.589 --> 00:07:04.860
YOSHI TAMURA: Domo arigato.

